63
00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:40.063
Cool, man.

64
00:02:40.063 --> 00:02:42.940
So feeling good, injury-free?

65
00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:43.917
Yeah, yeah, I'm much better now.

66
00:02:43.917 --> 00:02:46.420
I've been a bit more consistent with my running.

67
00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:49.140
I did, I wasn't able to run for three months,

68
00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.933
which killed me, because running is actually the thing

69
00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:54.383
that I enjoy the most outta training for Ironman.

70
00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:56.837
But yeah, so I'm back on course now

71
00:02:56.837 --> 00:02:58.860
and I'm ready to rock and roll in three week's time.

72
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.925
Right on, and you're 33 right now?

73
00:03:00.925 --> 00:03:01.758
How old are you?
I'm 35 now.

74
00:03:01.758 --> 00:03:03.760
35, 35.

75
00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:04.593
I'm getting on now.

76
00:03:04.593 --> 00:03:06.673
The gray hairs are coming out.

77
00:03:07.658 --> 00:03:11.453
And to date, what is your like best performance?

78
00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:13.710
My, probably my best performance

79
00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:17.063
would've been Ironman Frankfurt two years ago.

80
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.363
So basically when I started doing Ironman in 2014,

81
00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.450
I went for a stage where I went an hour faster

82
00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:28.380
every race I did, and that sort of really took hold

83
00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.280
when I did Ironman Frankfurt.

84
00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.650
It was a massive step up in my form,

85
00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:32.943
I did like nine hours.

86
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.077
I run nearly a sub three hour math

87
00:03:35.077 --> 00:03:36.477
and with the back end of it.

88
00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.910
Yeah, it was good and it was bad

89
00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:41.640
because the thing that kind of let me down

90
00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.430
was my right recycling a bit because I just lack

91
00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:47.710
that riding, 'cause I didn't stop riding until I was 30.

92
00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.650
So that technical element of riding a bike,

93
00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:53.280
and my cornering and breaking, and that sort of stuff

94
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.500
like it cost me a little bit.

95
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.520
Well, I mean 2014 was your first iron man.

96
00:03:58.520 --> 00:03:59.380
Yeah.

97
00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.380
So you're still branding with this whole thing.

98
00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.160
Yeah, yeah.

99
00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.200
It's a massive learning process that I've been going through

100
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.310
because it was frustrating as well.

101
00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:09.910
'Cause when I was doing my training

102
00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.660
and I was training on indoor, like static bikes,

103
00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:13.683
the numbers that I was generating,

104
00:04:13.683 --> 00:04:16.430
like power output and my physiology,

105
00:04:16.430 --> 00:04:18.840
like I know I was putting out the same sort of numbers

106
00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.020
like for FTP tests that at what a lot of sort of like

107
00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:24.530
decent pro iron man would be putting out.

108
00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.830
But I didn't seem to be getting that translation

109
00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:29.200
across into road cycling.

110
00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.400
Because again, I just lacked the experience

111
00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:34.410
of riding a bike and that's,

112
00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:35.453
I've had to obviously work on it a lot

113
00:04:35.453 --> 00:04:39.600
over the last few years and I progressively have got better,

114
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.110
but I used to be really, really bad.

115
00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:43.160
Like I used to be famous when we'd go on training camps,

116
00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:46.540
the Alps, I would descend on a bike quicker

117
00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:48.290
than I would climb up the other side of the mountain.

118
00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:50.080
'Cause I was just so frightened all the time,

119
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:51.053
holding the brakes.

120
00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.210
So that's amazing.

121
00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.470
There's a difference between the power

122
00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:59.210
that you can generate in a lab and then translating that

123
00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:01.700
into a real world context.

124
00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:02.923
So that's like the learning curve for you.

125
00:05:02.923 --> 00:05:03.913
It's totally different.

126
00:05:03.913 --> 00:05:06.940
Like obviously all of my training that I've ever done

127
00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:09.750
for obvious reasons has always been on static machines.

128
00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.700
So like the row machine years ago whilst I was in prison.

129
00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:15.193
And then when I come out of prison, I carried on trying.

130
00:05:15.193 --> 00:05:17.357
I tried to become a professional rower,

131
00:05:17.357 --> 00:05:18.720
but I took up that sport too late.

132
00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:21.070
And that's why I decided to do Ironman.

133
00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:26.070
So actually power generating on a static machine.

134
00:05:26.122 --> 00:05:27.250
You got that cover.

135
00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:28.400
I was very, very good light.

136
00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.360
What kind of watts were you putting out?

137
00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.140
So like I could hold like 365 watts

138
00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:37.367
when I was like 71 kilo for half hour.

139
00:05:37.367 --> 00:05:38.677
That's serious.

140
00:05:38.677 --> 00:05:39.870
For people that are listening

141
00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:41.660
that don't have a frame of reference,

142
00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:43.926
that's like world class.

143
00:05:43.926 --> 00:05:47.550
Obviously I was fortunate where the transition

144
00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:50.010
between rowing on a indoor row machine, that power,

145
00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.610
you tend to find a lot of rowers are very, very,

146
00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:54.670
very strong when they make the transition

147
00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:56.338
between rowing to cycling.

148
00:05:56.338 --> 00:05:58.530
So obviously where it's a leg dominated sport.

149
00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:00.280
And also, I just think you have the ability

150
00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:01.590
to be able to hurt yourself.

151
00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:04.970
Like I've never physically felt pain.

152
00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:08.170
Like I felt on an indoor row machine in an Ironman

153
00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:10.440
or cycling, nothing comes close,

154
00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.213
like to that when you are on the rivet. Right.

155
00:06:12.213 --> 00:06:14.150
Doing that a two K flat test,

156
00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:15.720
'cause it's like the lap tape builds up

157
00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:18.000
in every part of your body, your back, your legs,

158
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.620
your arms when you're on the row machine.

159
00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:24.940
And I think that, in thinking about your story,

160
00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:27.410
I mean, I think you have some genetic predisposition,

161
00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:29.750
like an ability to generate huge amounts of power,

162
00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:31.910
but your gift really is your ability

163
00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.950
to suffer over long periods of time.

164
00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:35.990
Yeah, yeah.

165
00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:39.620
Yeah, I would say that born through pain and life experience

166
00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:41.230
as much as anything else.

167
00:06:41.230 --> 00:06:43.793
Yeah, well like years ago,

168
00:06:43.793 --> 00:06:46.293
I wouldn't have seen it like that,

169
00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.400
but obviously some of the situations

170
00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.810
that I found myself in as a young man

171
00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.770
and some of the things that I did to myself,

172
00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:57.210
like when I was in prison, when I was a young,

173
00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:00.369
when a young teenager and I segregated myself

174
00:07:00.369 --> 00:07:03.720
in an isolation cell for a whole year,

175
00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.343
and that was like defiance against a system.

176
00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:09.460
The reason why I chose to do that was

177
00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:12.230
because I got moved to a prison and they tried

178
00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:13.880
to take all my clothes off me to put me

179
00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:15.950
in a special yellow boiler suit.

180
00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:17.500
So the prison officers could identify

181
00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:19.040
that I was an escape risk.

182
00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:23.060
And I was brought up to absolutely despise

183
00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.290
and hate the system and everything it stood for

184
00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.450
government, politicians, banks.

185
00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:29.510
And then when I found myself in prison,

186
00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:31.340
the system become very real.

187
00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:34.470
Those prison officers had locked me up were real people

188
00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:35.970
and they took my freedom away from me.

189
00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.690
And when they tried to take my clothes off me, I refuse.

190
00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:41.440
And they took me down to this isolation cell

191
00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:42.587
and they took my clothes off of me

192
00:07:42.587 --> 00:07:43.920
and they gave me this shitty she suit

193
00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:45.500
so the prison officers could identify

194
00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:47.380
that I was an escape risk in the prison.

195
00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.170
And then when the seven days was up,

196
00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:50.397
'cause that was what I was originally put in there

197
00:07:50.397 --> 00:07:51.230
for seven days up.

198
00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:52.290
Seven days of solitary.

199
00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:55.160
So solitary, they opened up the door and they said,

200
00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.190
you're gonna go back onto main allocation

201
00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:58.720
and you're gonna be a wining cleaner.

202
00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:02.630
And again, I hated these people and I said,

203
00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:05.620
there's no way I'm gonna go and do that.

204
00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:06.813
That I'm not going to do it.

205
00:08:06.813 --> 00:08:09.150
I'm not gonna clean up your rubbish every day.

206
00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:10.427
So he put me back in front of the governor

207
00:08:10.427 --> 00:08:11.420
and the governor said to me,

208
00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:13.400
you're refusing another law for order.

209
00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:14.233
And I said, yes.

210
00:08:14.233 --> 00:08:16.050
He said, well, you're going back in that cell

211
00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:16.883
for seven more days.

212
00:08:16.883 --> 00:08:19.800
And he gave me seven days confined to cell again.

213
00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:22.530
And then when I was in that process

214
00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:27.100
and I was sitting in that cell, I read about Nelson Mandela.

215
00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:29.810
And when I went to prison and I was a young man,

216
00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:32.780
I made a decision I would not become institutionalized.

217
00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:35.660
And when the librarian used to come around,

218
00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.810
this lady from the library used to come around a trolley

219
00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:38.700
and you take books off.

220
00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:41.150
And I used to think I would not become institutionalized.

221
00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:44.360
And I will educate myself to come out of here

222
00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:47.440
and be a better criminal, not to change, not to rehabilitate

223
00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.930
'cause I was brought up to believe that if you changed,

224
00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.520
you was weak and the system had broken you.

225
00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:58.290
So if you had rehabilitated and become a better person,

226
00:08:58.290 --> 00:08:59.170
You became soft.

227
00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:00.490
A criminal, wouldn't see it like that.

228
00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:02.480
They would see the system had broken you.

229
00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:04.720
So I had that mantra in my head.

230
00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:09.720
And when I was reading this book about Nelson Mandela

231
00:09:10.148 --> 00:09:12.983
and in the book, he said that when he was in prison,

232
00:09:12.983 --> 00:09:14.580
he used to smoke tobacco

233
00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:17.370
and he realized that the prison officers used to use that

234
00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:18.420
as a punishment against him.

235
00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:19.747
He stopped smoking cigarettes.

236
00:09:19.747 --> 00:09:22.970
So then my head as as, at this point I was 19.

237
00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:23.803
I thought,

238
00:09:23.803 --> 00:09:26.330
if you think by putting me in this isolation cell,

239
00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:28.800
that you're punishing me, I will take you away from you.

240
00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.900
So when they come up to seven days to put me back up on the

241
00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.510
wing, I refused.

242
00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:36.580
And then I stayed in that room for 365 days.

243
00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:37.970
By choice.
By choice.

244
00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:40.760
And they would come round Christmas.

245
00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.030
And I remember this and I look back on it now,

246
00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:44.630
like there was a prison officer.

247
00:09:45.910 --> 00:09:48.600
I didn't see it at the time. I hated him.

248
00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:50.540
But I remember he opened up cell Christmas

249
00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:52.880
and he said to me, he was just about to go off shift.

250
00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:54.470
And it was in the evening. It was about four o'clock.

251
00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:55.672
So obviously it was Christmas people got,

252
00:09:55.672 --> 00:09:57.573
I know earlier that day and he said to me,

253
00:09:57.573 --> 00:09:59.570
"Do you wanna phone up your mom?"

254
00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:00.403
And I said, "No."

255
00:10:00.403 --> 00:10:03.980
And I remember he opened up my cell door and he said, look,

256
00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:05.750
you can phone your mom up it's Christmas day.

257
00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:08.680
And I refused, I don't wanna use the phone.

258
00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:12.270
Because that would give them some tiny little bit

259
00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.150
of power, if they gave you something,

260
00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:15.330
they can take something away from.

261
00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:18.350
And I think it was me trying to wrestle

